.lua
book.chapter(19, "Imports, library discovery, and project layout")
.endlua
.sh 2 "A source import extends a session"
.pp
The form import "spec" loads a source unit into the current session. Resolution
tries the specification as a file, with an .ml suffix, and as a directory
containing mod.ml. This convention supports both single-file helpers and
module directories with adjacent tests. Import is a language operation with
evaluation consequences; it is not a textual include that merely pastes
characters before parsing.
.lua
book.code("metaml", [=[import "list"
Test.equal "imported traversal" (List.map (+ 1) [1, 2]) [2, 3]
Test.check "loader can resolve" (Option.is_some (Loader.resolve "list"))]=],
    "Import a standard-library source module")
.endlua
.pp
Search bases include the importing file's directory, the working directory,
embedding-configured search paths, and the selected standard-library
directory. A relative import should therefore be understood relative to its
loading context, not only to the shell command that eventually started the
interpreter. For a reproducible application, prefer a known project layout
and explicitly configured library paths over accidental working-directory
behavior.
.sh 2 "Successful imports are cached"
.pp
The loader tracks canonical paths so a successfully imported file normally
executes once. Different relative spellings that identify the same canonical
file should not create independent module universes. A path is inserted while
loading to prevent unbounded recursive import expansion. This breaks infinite
loading loops, but does not make cyclic imports equivalent to fully available
mutually recursive modules with all values initialized.
.lua
book.algorithm("Import a source unit", {
    "Resolve the specification against the available search bases.",
    "Canonicalize the selected file path.",
    "If already successfully loaded or currently loading, avoid recursive reload.",
    "Record the path and evaluate the source in the session.",
    "On success, retain the import-cache entry.",
    "On failure, remove the failed entry so a corrected file can be retried."
})
.endlua
.pp
Retryable loading is not transactional loading. Definitions and external
effects from earlier successful units of a failing file can remain. A retry
can therefore repeat some work even though the failed path was removed from
the cache. Organize source modules so loading defines pure interfaces and
functions, while a separate entry point performs effects. That architecture
reduces the number of partially completed actions a caller must reason about.
.sh 2 "Select the standard library deliberately"
.pp
Session.load_stdlib locates a tree containing prelude/mod.ml. Discovery can
consult METAML_STDLIB, a dialect-specific location under METAML_PREFIX,
paths relative to the executable, a configured default, and a working-directory
metalib fallback. Passing an explicit directory is the simplest reproducible
choice for examples and embedded deployments. The CLI does this when given
--stdlib, and the book checker always supplies the repository's library tree.
.lua
book.code("sh", [=[build/metaml-repl-cpp --stdlib /opt/myapp/metalib program.ml
METAML_STDLIB=/opt/myapp/metalib build/metaml-repl-cpp program.ml
# Inspect the interpreter's registered primitive boundary.
build/metaml-repl-cpp --stdlib metalib --list-primitives]=],
    "Explicit library selection and inspection")
.endlua
.pp
A fresh native Session does not silently execute external prelude files.
The host must request loading and check the result. This matters when an
application has no useful current directory or when installation is relocated.
Package the interpreter and library revision together. A source wrapper can
refer to a host primitive that is absent from an older executable, so copying
only new .ml files is not always a compatible upgrade.
.sh 2 "Separate portable source from native modules"
.pp
The portable metalib directories contain MetaML source used by both dialects.
Host modules under metalib/cpp and metalib/d add capabilities such as buffers,
structured-data parsers, graphs, and frontend tooling. Installing those host
modules establishes declarations and primitive implementations; it does not
grant permission to invoke every non-core primitive. Embeddings must choose
an allowlist separately. The developer CLI intentionally permits its installed
bundle for interactive work.
.pp
The files ending in .c under metalib/cpp are included C++ implementation
fragments, not C translation units. The genuine C-compatible interface lives
under metalib/c. Keep that distinction in build scripts and package manifests.
Likewise, a filename for a platform service is not proof that it belongs to
the portable bundle. Inspect its installer, dependencies, and mirrored
implementation before advertising it as available to every client.
.sh 2 "A practical project arrangement"
.pp
A small language tool can use a root entry file, a syntax module, a validation
module, an emission module, and fixtures. Put shared interfaces in a place
that does not depend on effectful startup. Arrange imports so the entry point
owns initialization order rather than each library importing the executable
driver. This makes a module independently testable and helps graph analysis
identify genuine cycles instead of artifacts of a tangled startup sequence.
.lua
book.code("text", [=[tool/
  main.ml          -- orchestration and explicit effects
  syntax/mod.ml    -- data model and constructors
  check/mod.ml     -- validation
  emit/mod.ml      -- rendering
  tests/           -- independent fixture programs]=], "A suggested application layout, not required directories")
.endlua
.pp
Use #load and #reload for deliberate interactive re-execution of a selected
file. Use import for stable dependency loading. The distinction is important
when a source edit appears to have no effect: an already imported path may
still be cached, while a reload intentionally runs again. A fresh session
is often the clearest way to verify a module's actual installation behavior
after changing its dependencies or public signature.
.sh 2 "Sources and further work"
.pp
Read the import resolver, Session.load_stdlib, and the Loader host module in
both dialects. CMakeLists.txt documents the installed standard-library paths
for this checkout. Create a two-module project with one shared type interface,
run it from a different working directory using explicit paths, and verify
that behavior is unchanged. Then introduce a failing imported unit and reason
about which earlier effects could remain after the error; design the entry
point so those effects are explicit and repeatable.
